WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
You are the Roastmaster General,

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.660
in not just the United States of America,

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.880
worldwide, (Jeff laughs) worldwide.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.350
And you know, it occurred to me,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.420
we should probably start
where all things must start,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.530
which is with Don Rickles.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.410
We lost Don a couple of years ago,

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.470
but you have picked up that mantle.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.840
I mean, you know, you are the guy

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.858
that everyone thinks of now

00:00:20.858 --> 00:00:22.470
when you think of insult comic,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.456
and you always have amazing jokes.

00:00:24.456 --> 00:00:26.010
Thank you.
You get terrific jokes.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:26.955
[Jeff] Thank you.

00:00:26.955 --> 00:00:27.788
Do you write them all?

00:00:27.788 --> 00:00:29.220
Do you have people that help you out?

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.130
My cousin Ed and my other buddy Ed,

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.800
and sometimes for the
big roasts, you know,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.580
full on writing staff will
help everyone on the dais.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.510
But I mostly try to do my own-

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.343
Right.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:45.240
And have, you know, a couple friends

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.350
who know me real well,

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.730
sit around with me and I
don't really accept jokes,

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.920
but I like to hone them with people.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.240
Yeah. Now, one of the things-

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.389
It sounds more personal
because if they sound,

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:59.340
what do you call it, recycled,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.190
they start to sound mean or predictable,

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.140
but I really want it to be like a suit

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.460
when I roast somebody,

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.140
tailor made just for them.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.077
Also, I think people appreciate that.

00:01:09.077 --> 00:01:12.240
If you're doing jokes about someone,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.400
and telling them they're so
fat that when they, you know,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.370
they need to leave the house
to, it's a standard joke.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.382
I'm gonna work on that one by the way.

00:01:18.382 --> 00:01:19.215
(group laugh)

00:01:19.215 --> 00:01:20.066
I know, that was rough.

00:01:20.066 --> 00:01:20.899
Is there an end?

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:21.732
That was rough.

00:01:21.732 --> 00:01:24.090
Oh, trust me. Oh, no, it's
going places, that joke.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.060
What Jeff will tell you is
that you start with that,

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.427
and then you work on it, but you-

00:01:30.427 --> 00:01:32.460
And thank you for that nice compliment

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:33.570
off the bat about Don.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.370
He really was the,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.590
what do they call it,
the sultan of insulting?

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.423
And -
Yes.

00:01:38.423 --> 00:01:40.170
He did teach me a lot.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.150
So I appreciate that.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.290
There was a few years there

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:47.040
where if you were a big
shot having a 50th birthday

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.260
or a 40th, you know, a big, big birthday,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.397
you'd have to have me and Don, you know?

00:01:51.397 --> 00:01:52.230
That's great.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.080
And he would inevitably go on after me

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.390
and make, I'd always write the jokes,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.220
and I'd always have papers of jokes.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.053
Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:01.650
And like John Stamos asked me

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.470
and Don Rickles to roast him for his 50th

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.730
at this fancy hotel

00:02:08.345 --> 00:02:09.480
and tuxedos and the whole thing.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.490
And I come in with pages
of stuff, you know,

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.260
John Stamos is so good looking,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.369
his birthday candles blew themselves.

00:02:15.369 --> 00:02:17.310
(group laugh)

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.810
And like, I really prepared,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.880
and John asked me to prepare.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.770
And then Rickles goes on after me,

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.900
and just makes fun of me for preparing.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.450
Yes. Well, this is what
I wanted to bring up,

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:28.980
the way in which you're
different from Rickles.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.000
Now I'll start with the day

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.940
I had an experience about 15 years ago.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.840
My wife and I are on a
flight cross country.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.490
We get on the plane.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.590
I'm putting my stuff in
the overhead compartment,

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.040
and I start to hear
someone say, "Jesus Christ

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.790
they'll let any guy on this plane."

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:47.466
(group laugh)

00:02:47.466 --> 00:02:48.357
And I'm like, I'm sort
of hearing it out of the,

00:02:48.357 --> 00:02:49.830
and I'm thinking, "What?"

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.530
And then he is like, Man,
Goddamn fucking Irish.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.410
They let the Irish on the plane
(group laughs) this far up?

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.030
You think the Irish
should be further back."

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.160
And I sort of start to get a little like,

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.510
I'm tired and I'm frustrated,

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.950
so I get irritated and I turn around.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:06.570
Don Rickles is sitting
right behind Liza and I.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.990
And he's sitting there,
and I swear the definition

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.600
of being happy as a pig in shit.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.360
My wife and I both, I
don't think I strapped

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.230
in the whole flight.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.840
Both of our heads are
peeking over behind us,

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.600
and the whole flight he's going after me,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.160
and then he is going after, you know,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:23.993
what are your plans?

00:03:23.993 --> 00:03:25.830
We told him, he went after our plans.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.210
Then he starts going after Newhart,

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.380
who he was best friends with,

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.460
who he knew that I knew.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.293
Yeah.

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:31.980
And just on and on and on.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.373
And that flight felt like
it was 20 seconds long.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.470
But here's what I've always thought

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.790
about what's interesting,

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.820
'cause I, for lack of
anything better to do,

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.233
I think about comedy a lot,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.130
and I was thinking about you this morning.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.400
You are someone who,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.500
and I give you a lot of credit for this.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.990
I'm a big believer in preparation.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:58.470
I think you prepare very, very, very well.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.350
I think you take these
things really seriously,

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.750
and your jokes are very crafted.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.690
I'm gonna say that and I love Don.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.300
Don had a different technique.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.520
He was a nerve comic in a lot of ways.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.970
He wouldn't, I mean, there's
so many times I saw him.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.106
If you saw him like on
a Dean Martin roast,

00:04:16.106 --> 00:04:17.340
yes, he had material,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.150
but so often he would just
go by the seat of his pants,

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:25.140
and it wasn't so much
crafted, intricate insults.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:25.973
Do you know what I mean?

00:04:25.973 --> 00:04:27.060
It was a-
In the moment.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.613
It was a full on bear
attack in the moment.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.430
And if you went back and
looked at the transcript,

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.004
you'd say some of this doesn't
make any sense, you know,

00:04:36.004 --> 00:04:37.200
"Look at this guy over here," you know

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.700
and he would come on my show and do stuff.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.920
And every now and then,
one of my favorite things

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.840
that he used to say
was you'd say something

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:43.673
and he'd be like, "Oh, good.

00:04:43.673 --> 00:04:44.506
Good for you, smart guy.

00:04:44.506 --> 00:04:45.339
What do you want, a cookie?"

00:04:45.339 --> 00:04:46.287
And I just thought, (group laugh)

00:04:46.287 --> 00:04:48.570
and I think I just laughed
cuz he said, "Cookie.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.100
What do you want, a cookie?"

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.480
Yeah. His timing.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.480
It was just the way he said it.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.770
And, you know, he did chastise me

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:56.910
and I did get embarrassed,

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:58.950
you know like, "Oh God,
I'm the kid who did

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:03.950
all the homework and he would
go up after me and crush,

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.620
and it always kind of bothered me.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.250
Maybe my skin wasn't thick
enough to take it from him.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:15.810
And then a couple years
after that Stamos thing,

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.830
I was at Don's Memorial at a synagogue,

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:21.720
and you know, all the
comics were sitting there,

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.120
we're all invited and
remember Judd Apatow's

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.040
right in front of me, and
all the guys are there.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.040
And Don's manager, Tony,
comes walking over to me

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.891
and he says, "Jeff, can you speak?"

00:05:34.891 --> 00:05:37.980
This is something I would've
prepared a month for,

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:38.853
had I known.

00:05:39.843 --> 00:05:42.180
And I look at Judd,

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.180
I go, "This is Don challenging
me right now to talk."

00:05:47.953 --> 00:05:49.650
No papers kid.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:50.483
And I did it.

00:05:50.483 --> 00:05:52.560
I just stood up and I took the mic,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.690
and handled my business for five minutes.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.920
And it was funny and it was sweet.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.190
And I remember being like,
"Freaking Don taught me something

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.500
from the grave, that I can do it."

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:02.857
And somewhere from the beyond he's like,

00:06:02.857 --> 00:06:04.326
"What do you want, a cookie?"

00:06:04.326 --> 00:06:05.226
(group laugh)

00:06:05.226 --> 00:06:06.390
And I do always want a cookie,

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:07.953
so that did work out.

